Usage:  XYDown <opts> <filenames>Command line options:    -t <path>   Use "path" as the serial port.  Defaults to /t2    -a          Interpret the file(s) being downloaded as ASCII.    -b          Interpret the file(s) being downloaded as Binary.    -r <rate>   Use <rate> as the baud rate.  i.e. -r 300  for 300 baud.    -l <path>   Use <path> as the path for logging information    -d          Set debugging messages on.   If no filenames are specified, XYDown will use the filenamespecified by YModem-Batch, or "xmodem.file" if the download isplain XModem or YModem.Description   XYDown is a standalone program for downloading files usingXModem, YModem or YModem-batch.  It is designed to be used inconjunction with a separate terminal program.  To use XYDown,you should fork a shell from inside the terminal program andrun XYDown from there.  Alternatively, some terminal programs(notably KBCom) have the ability to directly run separate programs.   XYDown will automatically do the following:   - Determine the protocol being used by the sender   - Determine whether a file being downloaded is ASCII or binary     (this can be overridden by specifying -a or -b on the command line)   - Convert the end-of-line markers in ASCII files     (i.e. removing linefeeds if necessary)   - Avoid over-writing existing files by altering the filename   When run with no options, XYDown will set up the following display:   Blks Bytes FileSize Errs CErrs Protocol Type Filename     0      0     ???    0     0   ???     ???  ??? - Blks is a count of the number of blocks received so far. - Bytes is a count of the total number of bytes received so far. - FileSize is the YModem-Batch file size.  It shows "???" if the   transfer is not a YModem-Batch transfer or if no filesize was   specified by the sender. - Errs is a count of the total number of errors to have occurred so far. - CErrs is the number of consecutive errors that have just occured.   This number is set to zero after each successfully received packet. - The Protocol field shows the protocol currently in use.  It will   typically change several times at the beginning of each file transferred,   as XYDown determines the actual protocol in use. - The Type field shows the current file type (ASC or BIN).  If   XYDown's automatic file-type determination is in use, then this   field will show "???" until approximately 1k of the file has been   received, at which time XYDown will attempt to guess the filetype. - The Filename shows the filename currently being used.  This can   sometimes change two or more times as XYDown changes the name in   order to avoid over-writing files.Usage with KBCom:   Adding the following line to any ".kbc" configuration file willallow Alt-D to automatically run XYDown (for KBCom 1.0 or 1.1):ALT_D = "xydown -t %t  -r %b ", "YNYY", 2,2, 76,6It will first give you a chance to type in filenames for the download,or -a or -b to force the filetype, and will then proceed.Caveats:   The automatic file-type determination has worked well for me so far.If it gets something wrong, just <BREAK> out of XYDown (it will waita few seconds to try to get things in a sane state), type Ctrl-X severaltimes to stop the other end, and try again, specifying -a or -b.   Just for reference, the algorithm used is as follows.  Tell me ifyou have any improvements:   Several checks are made on the first 1k of the file.  Failing anyone of them will mark the file as binary.  The checks are:   - The first byte cannot be null or Ctrl-Z.   - There can be no bytes with the high bit set.   - Only the following control characters may appear:      Null, Tab, LF, CR, and Ctrl-Z.   - Only one of Ctrl-Z and Null may appear, and then only at the      end of the tested section of the file.The special consideration given to Null and Ctrl-Z attempts to allow fortheir common usage as XModem pad characters.Public Domain:   XYDown and it's source are released into the public domain.  Thismeans that anyone can use this program or it's source for any reasonat all, with no legal obligations whatsoever.  In particular, I hopethat authors of terminal programs will use these ideas (or even thiscode!) in their programs.  There is now no excuse at all for an OS9terminal program to not fully support YModem Batch!  <grin>  In thesimplest usage, a terminal program author may simply elect to distributeXYDown with their program, and have their program Fork XYDown to handlethe download.  Alternatively, they can study and use any or all ofthis source in their programs.